LEVEL 2 → 3 — READING A FILE WITH SPACES IN ITS NAME


WHAT THIS LEVEL IS ABOUT :

The password is in a file named spaces in this filename. Shells use spaces as argument separators, so without proper handling, each word is interpreted as a separate argument. This level teaches quoting and escaping.


APPROACH : 

After logging in as bandit2, running cat --spaces in this filename predictably failed — the shell split it into multiple arguments. The correct approach was to quote the entire filename:
cat "./--spaces in this filename--"

Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]


KEY TAKEAWAY : 

Wrapping a filename in double quotes forces the shell to treat the entire string as a single argument. Alternatively, each space can be escaped with a backslash: cat spaces\ in\ this\ filename. Tab completion handles this automatically and is the fastest approach in practice.
